today marks our state's first day of  early voting for the 2020 general  election  there are 1.2 million nevadans  registered to vote this year  at our last check this afternoon clark  county reported nearly 18  thousand voters have cast their ballots  so far  as kate houston reports many voters  started to line up before those polls  even opened  the las vegas athletic club in the  northwest is one of 48 early voting  sites and as you can see behind me  we have a line of voters here waiting  patiently to get inside  a long line of early voters wrapped  through the lvac parking lot off of  rainbow and lake mead  many brought chairs snacks and music as  they waited some tell me they started  lining up well before the polls opened  we put our chairs out we had coffee  and we sat and talked until they opened  up during the 14 days of early voting  registered voters can send in their mail  ballot drop it off at a voting site or  head inside one of these tents to vote  in person  the clark county election department  says this ballot is the longest ever  with 70 contests and five ballot  questions  it was a large ballot so what we did was  filled it out at home  so when we came here we wouldn't hold up  the lines and stuff to let other people  be able to vote  there were many events to kick off early  voting governor steve sisselak went to  the boulevard mall to drop off his mail  ballot with his family and address  voters  decide what you're going to do are you  going to drop it off you're going to  mail it in you're going to go to an  early voting site  make a plan with your family and go  together governor sissellack addressed  the long lines saying there's been a  great turnout  we know that there's going to be some  lines and that's a good thing for us we  ask you to be patient  and take the time to exercise your right  early voting will last through  october 30th kate houston 8 news now  the clark county elections department is  reminding voters that mass  and social distancing are required at  all polling places  there will be additional staff to  sanitize voting machines and other areas  after each use
 

